A Boolean word lies in wordsUpTo n if and only if its length is at most n.

The proof inducts on n, keeping the word general. At zero, membership is membership in the singleton of the empty word: the forward direction simplifies the empty word, and the reverse direction uses that a word of length at most zero is empty. At a successor, membership is rewritten as membership in the appended list or as a flatMap witness. A witness from the previous list inherits the successor bound from the inductive hypothesis. A flatMap witness is a previous word extended by false or by true, and the successor length bound follows from the inductive hypothesis. Conversely, if the length is already at most n, the inductive hypothesis gives the left disjunct. Otherwise the empty word is impossible, so the word is a head and a tail; the tail has length at most n and lies in the previous list, and the head false or true places the word in the corresponding extension.
